1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,933
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,953 --> 00:00:10,049
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:10,449 --> 00:00:16,229
This is Lecture 30, trends in sewing equipment.

4
00:00:16,629 --> 00:00:21,510
Last time we saw how five kinds of factory use their machines.

5
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:23,882
Today we look ten years ahead,

6
00:00:23,902 --> 00:00:29,969
at which changes are already happening and which still have hurdles to clear.

7
00:00:30,369 --> 00:00:35,529
This video concentrates on saving power and robot loading;

8
00:00:35,549 --> 00:00:42,674
software-defined machines and garments without stitches get only a mention here,

9
00:00:42,694 --> 00:00:45,442
with the details in the slides.

10
00:00:46,962 --> 00:00:48,862
Here's the problem.

11
00:00:49,262 --> 00:00:56,123
A shirt factory is considering a robot to take cut pieces from a stack to the

12
00:00:56,143 --> 00:00:57,464
sewing machine:

13
00:00:57,484 --> 00:01:02,379
separate a ply, lift it, flatten it, align it, load it,

14
00:01:02,399 --> 00:01:05,060
ten actions of 8 seconds each,

15
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:09,707
and the supplier quotes 97 percent success per step.

16
00:01:10,107 --> 00:01:17,623
Each failure takes a person 30 seconds to sort out, then the step is redone.

17
00:01:18,023 --> 00:01:21,582
The robot costs 250 thousand yuan,

18
00:01:21,602 --> 00:01:26,424
maintenance is 10 percent of the price a year,

19
00:01:26,444 --> 00:01:33,160
it replaces one operator per shift, two shifts, 300 days a year,

20
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:36,107
labour at 0.5 yuan a minute.

21
00:01:36,507 --> 00:01:39,930
What's the chance the whole sequence runs unattended?

22
00:01:40,330 --> 00:01:42,990
How often per hour must a person step in?

23
00:01:43,390 --> 00:01:44,699
How long to pay back?

24
00:01:45,099 --> 00:01:46,679
We'll answer at the end.

25
00:01:48,199 --> 00:01:49,859
Four directions.

26
00:01:51,379 --> 00:01:58,207
Four columns: leaner, smarter, fewer hands, no stitches; in each,

27
00:01:58,227 --> 00:02:01,999
the lower the newer and less mature.

28
00:02:02,399 --> 00:02:06,939
Leaner is the most mature: servo direct drive.

29
00:02:07,339 --> 00:02:13,990
Smarter means electronic feed, tension and presser foot,

30
00:02:14,010 --> 00:02:18,279
plus connectivity and digital twins.

31
00:02:18,679 --> 00:02:24,976
Fewer hands runs from pattern sewers and automatic units to robot loading and

32
00:02:24,996 --> 00:02:26,699
then humanoid robots.

33
00:02:27,099 --> 00:02:31,728
No stitches means whole-garment knitting, welding and bonding,

34
00:02:31,748 --> 00:02:33,303
and digital printing.

35
00:02:33,703 --> 00:02:38,867
We'll spend most time on fewer hands, because two things hold it back:

36
00:02:38,887 --> 00:02:42,643
fabric is soft, and the investment has to pay back.

37
00:02:44,163 --> 00:02:47,103
The most mature trend is saving power.

38
00:02:47,503 --> 00:02:52,809
An old clutch motor spins at rated speed whenever it's switched on,

39
00:02:52,829 --> 00:02:58,452
and the pedal only engages the clutch; a servo turns only while sewing,

40
00:02:58,472 --> 00:03:01,314
and direct drive drops the belt too.

41
00:03:01,714 --> 00:03:10,308
A published retrofit cut average power from about 450 to 250 watts,

42
00:03:10,328 --> 00:03:16,094
about 44 percent, paying back in four months.

43
00:03:16,494 --> 00:03:22,255
Scale to 500 machines running 3550 hours a year:

44
00:03:22,275 --> 00:03:30,928
about 80 ten-thousand kilowatt-hours with clutch motors, 44 with servos,

45
00:03:30,948 --> 00:03:37,794
saving 35.5 ten-thousand a year, about 202 tonnes of CO2.

46
00:03:38,194 --> 00:03:41,454
The more idle time, the bigger the saving.

47
00:03:42,973 --> 00:03:48,993
How soft is cloth? It won't stand, it can't be pushed.

48
00:03:50,513 --> 00:03:55,762
Why is handling cloth so hard for robots?

49
00:03:56,162 --> 00:03:58,598
First measure how soft it is.

50
00:03:58,998 --> 00:04:00,868
The cantilever test:

51
00:04:00,888 --> 00:04:07,388
slide a strip out over the table edge until it droops to a set angle;

52
00:04:07,408 --> 00:04:10,978
that overhang is the bending length c.

53
00:04:11,378 --> 00:04:16,718
Bending rigidity is weight per area times c cubed.

54
00:04:17,118 --> 00:04:27,319
Single jersey is about 4, shirt poplin about 9.4, suiting about 38,

55
00:04:27,339 --> 00:04:32,658
denim about 168 micronewton-metres.

56
00:04:33,058 --> 00:04:39,190
A one-millimetre steel plate is 16.7 newton-metres,

57
00:04:39,210 --> 00:04:44,738
roughly 1.8 million times stiffer than poplin.

58
00:04:45,138 --> 00:04:48,638
Robots are used to handling steel, not cloth.

59
00:04:50,158 --> 00:04:51,421
Watch the lab:

60
00:04:51,441 --> 00:04:58,388
a strip sticks out 10 centimetres from the clamp while its bending length is

61
00:04:58,408 --> 00:05:01,871
raised from jersey-soft to card-stiff;

62
00:05:01,891 --> 00:05:05,538
the orange line is the current material.

63
00:05:05,938 --> 00:05:10,002
With small c the whole strip hangs down:

64
00:05:10,022 --> 00:05:16,229
jersey and poplin droop more than 80 degrees, denim about 65,

65
00:05:16,249 --> 00:05:19,598
while card and steel barely move.

66
00:05:19,998 --> 00:05:28,441
At an overhang of about twice the bending length the chord angle is about 42

67
00:05:28,461 --> 00:05:29,332
degrees,

68
00:05:29,352 --> 00:05:32,896
which is how the test defines c.

69
00:05:33,296 --> 00:05:38,916
Clamped upright, shirt fabric stands only about 4 centimetres.

70
00:05:39,316 --> 00:05:44,936
Lift cloth off the table and it stops doing what you want.

71
00:05:46,456 --> 00:05:48,346
Cloth can't be pushed either.

72
00:05:48,746 --> 00:05:55,838
A strip clamped at one end and free to move sideways at the other behaves

73
00:05:55,858 --> 00:05:57,981
like a slender column:

74
00:05:58,001 --> 00:06:02,755
push it to the critical force F_cr and it arches.

75
00:06:03,155 --> 00:06:12,963
F_cr is pi squared times B times width b, over four times length squared.

76
00:06:13,363 --> 00:06:20,297
Shirt poplin 5 centimetres long and 30 wide buckles at about 2.8 millinewtons,

77
00:06:20,317 --> 00:06:27,161
while it weighs about 18: you can't even push with a sixth of its own weight.

78
00:06:27,561 --> 00:06:35,476
To push harder than its weight, c must exceed 0.74 L: for 5 centimetres,

79
00:06:35,496 --> 00:06:40,435
more than 37 millimetres, stiffer than denim.

80
00:06:40,835 --> 00:06:47,071
That's why a sewing machine drags fabric with its feed dog instead of pushing it.

81
00:06:48,591 --> 00:06:54,505
Robot loading: check the numbers, then compute pⁿ.

82
00:06:56,025 --> 00:06:58,005
Here's a public case.

83
00:06:58,405 --> 00:07:07,145
In 2017 a sewing-robot company and an apparel maker announced a plant with 21

84
00:07:07,165 --> 00:07:10,558
fully automatic T-shirt lines,

85
00:07:10,578 --> 00:07:15,905
one shirt every 22 seconds, 800 thousand a day.

86
00:07:16,305 --> 00:07:26,838
Do the sums: 86400 seconds a day over 22 is about 3927 per line,

87
00:07:26,858 --> 00:07:39,205
so 21 lines make about 82 thousand, roughly 10 times short of 800 thousand.

88
00:07:39,605 --> 00:07:41,980
The numbers contradict themselves.

89
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:44,526
The plan never happened,

90
00:07:44,546 --> 00:07:51,026
and reports in 2025 said round-the-clock high speed with yields above 98

91
00:07:51,046 --> 00:07:53,012
percent is still hard;

92
00:07:53,032 --> 00:07:59,240
a new batch of cotton or a new dye lot means stopping to recalibrate.

93
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:04,835
When you read about new technology, check the numbers first.

94
00:08:06,355 --> 00:08:13,091
A process has n actions, each succeeding with probability p, independently;

95
00:08:13,111 --> 00:08:18,135
the chance it finishes with no human help is p to the n.

96
00:08:18,535 --> 00:08:26,640
The lab cycles the per-step success from 0.90 to 0.999;

97
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:30,629
watch the dot at ten steps.

98
00:08:31,029 --> 00:08:35,094
0.97 to the tenth is about 74 percent,

99
00:08:35,114 --> 00:08:39,609
so about one piece in four needs a person.

100
00:08:40,009 --> 00:08:45,970
At 99 percent per step, the whole is about 90 percent.

101
00:08:46,370 --> 00:08:55,750
For 90 percent unattended over ten steps you need about 98.95 percent per step.

102
00:08:56,150 --> 00:09:01,967
A respectable-looking 97 falls short once you multiply.

103
00:09:03,487 --> 00:09:06,102
Now the interventions.

104
00:09:06,502 --> 00:09:14,632
Expected failures per piece are n times one minus p, over p:

105
00:09:14,652 --> 00:09:20,202
ten steps at 97 percent gives about 0.31.

106
00:09:20,602 --> 00:09:27,224
With 8-second steps and 30 seconds of human fixing per failure plus a redo,

107
00:09:27,244 --> 00:09:32,094
the cycle is about 92 seconds, about 39 pieces an hour,

108
00:09:32,114 --> 00:09:39,091
and about 12.1 interventions an hour: someone has to walk over every 5 minutes,

109
00:09:39,111 --> 00:09:42,102
so a person can't leave the robot.

110
00:09:42,502 --> 00:09:48,962
At 99 percent it's about 4.3 an hour; at 99.9, under half.

111
00:09:49,362 --> 00:09:54,721
Only beyond 99 percent per step can the robot be left alone.

112
00:09:56,241 --> 00:10:03,286
The buckling formula is exactly the problem robots face assembling wiring

113
00:10:03,306 --> 00:10:05,221
harnesses and hoses.

114
00:10:05,621 --> 00:10:09,304
When a gripper pushes a wire into a connector,

115
00:10:09,324 --> 00:10:14,940
the unsupported length of wire is a slender column clamped at one end.

116
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:20,366
The critical force goes inversely with the square of that length,

117
00:10:20,386 --> 00:10:25,800
so gripping twice as close to the tip lets you push four times harder.

118
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:32,850
That's why harness robots grip near the tip and push in several short strokes,

119
00:10:32,870 --> 00:10:34,218
or pull instead.

120
00:10:34,618 --> 00:10:41,258
And the p-to-the-n rule applies to bin picking too: recognition, grasping,

121
00:10:41,278 --> 00:10:46,478
pose correction and placing, each success rate multiplied.

122
00:10:47,998 --> 00:10:48,921
Try it at home.

123
00:10:49,321 --> 00:10:54,223
Pull a towel by one corner and it slides flat across the table after you;

124
00:10:54,243 --> 00:10:57,325
push it from behind and it bunches up at once.

125
00:10:57,725 --> 00:11:01,099
That's the buckling formula we just saw:

126
00:11:01,119 --> 00:11:07,633
cloth's bending rigidity is so small that it arches before the push reaches a

127
00:11:07,653 --> 00:11:09,585
fraction of its weight.

128
00:11:09,985 --> 00:11:16,296
And it's why a sewing machine's feed dog drags the fabric from underneath

129
00:11:16,316 --> 00:11:19,245
instead of pushing it from behind.

130
00:11:20,765 --> 00:11:23,288
Back to our problem.

131
00:11:23,688 --> 00:11:32,653
The chance the whole sequence runs unattended is 0.97 to the tenth,

132
00:11:32,673 --> 00:11:39,761
about 73.7 percent: one piece in four needs a person.

133
00:11:40,161 --> 00:11:47,778
Expected failures are about 0.31 a piece, the cycle about 92 seconds,

134
00:11:47,798 --> 00:11:54,420
output about 39 an hour, and about 12 interventions an hour.

135
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:59,607
Labour saved is 14.4 ten-thousand yuan a year;

136
00:11:59,627 --> 00:12:03,683
after failure handling and maintenance,

137
00:12:03,703 --> 00:12:09,640
payback is about 29 months on two shifts and 18 on three.

138
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:15,427
For 90 percent unattended you need about 99 percent per step,

139
00:12:15,447 --> 00:12:19,327
which cuts interventions to about 4 an hour.

140
00:12:19,727 --> 00:12:26,747
The supplier's 97 sounds high, but multiplied it isn't enough.

141
00:12:28,267 --> 00:12:32,293
Now Virtual lab 30-1, soft fabric and robots,

142
00:12:32,313 --> 00:12:36,788
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. The checks,

143
00:12:36,808 --> 00:12:43,441
the payback chart and the tasks are blurred. Page one, how soft is fabric:

144
00:12:43,461 --> 00:12:49,644
pick the four fabrics, card and steel in turn from the material list,

145
00:12:49,664 --> 00:12:52,341
with a 10-centimetre overhang.

146
00:12:52,361 --> 00:12:58,545
Watch the orange line go from hanging straight down to barely moving,

147
00:12:58,565 --> 00:13:01,781
and record each material's rigidity,

148
00:13:01,801 --> 00:13:06,007
chord angle and the force that buckles a strip.

149
00:13:06,407 --> 00:13:08,520
Back on shirting poplin,

150
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:13,409
drag only the weight and see whether the droop changes;

151
00:13:13,429 --> 00:13:19,187
then drag only the bending length, and compare which one matters.

152
00:13:19,587 --> 00:13:22,624
Tasks one and two are on this page too:

153
00:13:22,644 --> 00:13:28,582
one asks you to bring the chord angle under 10 degrees and work out how much

154
00:13:28,602 --> 00:13:29,837
stiffer that is;

155
00:13:29,857 --> 00:13:35,089
the other changes the strip width, checks the push-to-weight ratio,

156
00:13:35,109 --> 00:13:39,087
and finds the bending length at which you can push.

157
00:13:39,487 --> 00:13:42,097
Page two is the robot workflow.

158
00:13:42,117 --> 00:13:47,357
Start from ten steps at 97 percent and record unattended rate,

159
00:13:47,377 --> 00:13:51,345
interventions and output per hour, and payback.

160
00:13:51,365 --> 00:13:58,387
Task three asks for the lowest per-step success that reaches 90 percent unattended.

161
00:13:58,787 --> 00:14:05,783
Task four keeps two shifts, at most 97 percent and at least ten steps,

162
00:14:05,803 --> 00:14:08,490
and asks you to tune price,

163
00:14:08,510 --> 00:14:16,107
wage or step time until payback is 24 months or less; then try three shifts.

164
00:14:16,507 --> 00:14:22,235
Page three is the direct-drive energy account. Record the plant's annual use,

165
00:14:22,255 --> 00:14:27,311
saving and emissions at the defaults, then change the running hours;

166
00:14:27,331 --> 00:14:30,595
task five asks for a set saving per machine.

167
00:14:30,615 --> 00:14:34,327
The guide and report template are on the platform.

168
00:14:35,847 --> 00:14:42,565
Everything in this lecture is on the WenQuest learning platform.

169
00:14:42,965 --> 00:14:50,211
You can open the interactive slides and the soft-fabric lab and work them yourself,

170
